+\chapter{Central operations and compatible boundary homotopies}
+\label{ch:cc-boundary}
+
+A scalar can act trivially on the cohomology of every boundary
+while remaining nonzero when compatibility with boundary maps
+is retained. The distinction can already be seen in two-by-two
+polynomial matrices.
+
+Let \(k\) be a field of characteristic zero and put \(R=k[x]\).
+For the potential \(W=x^3\), consider the boundary
+\[
+ D=\begin{pmatrix}0&x\\x^2&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad D^2=x^3\,1.
+\]
+Its endomorphisms form the parity-graded algebra \(A\) of
+two-by-two polynomial matrices. Diagonal matrices are even,
+off-diagonal matrices are odd, and
+\(\delta f=Df-(-1)^{|f|}fD\).
+The square of \(\delta\) is zero because \(D^2\) is scalar.
+The odd matrices
+\[
+ H=\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
+ e=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-x&0\end{pmatrix}
+\]
+satisfy
+\begin{equation}
+ \delta H=x\,1,\qquad \delta e=0,\qquad He+eH=1.
+ \label{eq:cc-cubic-opening}
+\end{equation}
+Thus \(H\) makes multiplication by \(x\) null-homotopic on this
+boundary. Its anticommutator with the closed odd operator \(e\)
+is the identity. The identity is not a boundary in \(A\):
+every entry of \(\delta f\) is divisible by \(x\).
+The homotopy \(H\) therefore has a nonzero obstruction to
+compatibility with \(e\).
+
+Compatibility introduces a new equation for each string of
+boundary maps. Hochschild cochains assemble these equations,
+including their signs, into one differential. The diagonal
+contraction gives explicit solutions for the action of every
+closed diagonal operator.
+
+\section{The coefficient ring in a boundary comparison}
+\label{sec:cc-coefficients}
+
+For \(n\geq1\), let \(R=k[x_1,\ldots,x_n]\), let \(W\in R\),
+and fix \(c\in k\). Let \(\mathcal C\) have as objects finite
+free, based matrix factorizations \(E\) of \(W-c\).
+Its maps \(f:E\to F\) are all homogeneous \(R\)-linear matrices,
+with
+\[
+ \delta f=D_Ff-(-1)^{|f|}fD_E.
+\]
+Composition is matrix multiplication. The product rule for
+\(\delta\) makes \(\mathcal C\) a differential graded category.
+It is both \(R\)-linear and \(k\)-linear. For the cochains below,
+multilinearity in a string of maps means multilinearity over \(k\).
+Their values are still \(R\)-linear boundary maps.
+
+Write \(S=k[x,y]\), where \(x,y\) each denote \(n\) coordinates.
+The diagonal factorization \(\Delta_W\), its action
+\(M_E=\Delta_W\otimes_{k[y]}E(y)\), and the maps
+\(\pi_E,i_E,h_E\) are those of Theorem~\ref{thm:da-unit}.
+In particular,
+\[
+ \delta h_E=1-i_E\pi_E,\qquad
+ \pi_Ei_E=1,\qquad h_E^2=\pi_Eh_E=h_Ei_E=0.
+\]
+Tensoring a boundary map gives
+\[
+ \mathcal T(f)=1_{\Delta_W}\otimes f(y).
+\]
+The tensor sign rule applies to odd \(f\). This construction is
+a \(k\)-linear differential graded functor, with
+\[
+ \pi_F\mathcal T(f)=f(x)\pi_E.
+\]
+As a map between spaces of boundary maps, \(\mathcal T\) is
+generally not \(R\)-linear for the left \(x\)-action. Indeed,
+\[
+ \mathcal T(x_jf)=y_j\mathcal T(f),\qquad
+ x_j\mathcal T(f)-\mathcal T(x_jf)
+       =(x_j-y_j)\mathcal T(f).
+\]
+For \(f=1_E\) and \(E\ne0\), the last polynomial operator is
+nonzero. Evaluation at \(y=x\) removes this difference. A
+cochain construction before evaluation must retain it.
+
+\section{The equations for coherent centrality}
+\label{sec:cc-cochains}
+
+The suspension of a parity-graded space is \(sV\), with
+\(|sv|=|v|+1\). Order a composable string from left to right as
+\[
+ f_j:E_j\longrightarrow E_{j-1}\quad(1\leq j\leq m).
+\]
+Thus its composite is \(f_1\cdots f_m:E_m\to E_0\).
+Let \(\mathcal B\mathcal C\) be the vector space of finite
+linear combinations of suspended composable words
+\(sf_1\otimes\cdots\otimes sf_m\), including an empty word at
+each object. Splitting at every position gives its
+deconcatenation coproduct, with the intervening object retained.
+
+A suspended Hochschild cochain \(U\) of parity \(q\) is a family
+of \(k\)-multilinear maps
+\[
+ U_m:
+ s\mathcal C(E_1,E_0)\otimes_k\cdots\otimes_k
+ s\mathcal C(E_m,E_{m-1})
+ \longrightarrow s\mathcal C(E_m,E_0)
+\]
+of parity \(q\), for all strings and all \(m\geq0\).
+For \(m=0\), this is a choice of an element of
+\(s\mathcal C(E,E)\) at every object \(E\).
+The space of cochains is the product of these spaces over
+arities and object strings.
+
+Extend \(U\) to words by insertion into every consecutive block.
+An insertion after the first \(j\) letters has sign
+\[
+ (-1)^{q\sum_{i=1}^j(|f_i|+1)}.
+\]
+A nullary component is inserted at a gap, including either end,
+using the object at that gap. Denote this extension by
+\(\widehat U\). Each input word permits only finitely many
+insertions, even if \(U\) has infinitely many components.
+The insertion formula satisfies the coderivation rule for
+deconcatenation.
+
+Define the odd coderivation \(b\) by
+\[
+ b_1(sf)=s\,\delta f,\qquad
+ b_2(sf\otimes sg)=(-1)^{|f|}s(fg),\qquad b_0=0,
+\]
+with all higher components zero. Its square vanishes:
+the length-one contribution is \(\delta^2\), the length-two
+contribution is the product rule, and the length-three
+contribution is associativity. Disjoint insertions cancel
+with the opposite order of insertion.
+
+The differential of a cochain is the length-one projection of
+\begin{equation}
+ \widehat{\mathfrak d U}
+      =b\widehat U-(-1)^q\widehat U b .
+ \label{eq:cc-differential}
+\end{equation}
+The commutator of coderivations is again a coderivation, as
+expansion of their coproducts shows. Thus the right side is
+determined by that projection. Expanding its commutator with
+\(b\) gives
+\(\widehat{\mathfrak d^2 U}=b^2\widehat U-\widehat U b^2=0\).
+This defines a cochain complex.
+
+A central operation of parity \(p\), with all its homotopies,
+means a cochain \(U\) of parity \(p+1\) with
+\(\mathfrak dU=0\). Two such operations give the same class
+when their difference is \(\mathfrak dV\).
+With this shift, the parity-\(p\) Hochschild cohomology is
+\(HH_k^{\bar p}(\mathcal C)=H^{\overline{p+1}}(\mathfrak C)\),
+where \(\mathfrak C\) denotes the displayed suspended cochain
+complex.
+
+The nullary equation says that \(U_0\) is closed. If all higher
+components vanish, the unary equation says that the resulting
+boundary endomorphisms graded-commute with every map. A nonzero
+unary component supplies the homotopy for that equation.
+The binary equation then requires compatibility with composition,
+and higher arities impose its successive compatibility equations.
+This definition includes arbitrary, possibly nonclosed maps.
+
+\section{The diagonal cochain on every boundary string}
+\label{sec:cc-comparison}
+
+Put \(B_W=\operatorname{End}_S(\Delta_W)\), with differential
+\(\delta a=D_\Delta a-(-1)^{|a|}aD_\Delta\).
+For each object \(E\), let \(L_E(a)=a\otimes1_E\) on \(M_E\).
+For \(a\) homogeneous of parity \(p\),
+\[
+ L_E(a)\mathcal T(f)=(-1)^{p|f|}
+                  \mathcal T(f)L_F(a)
+ \quad\text{when } f:F\to E.
+\]
+Both sides are maps \(M_F\to M_E\), and the equality is the
+tensor sign rule.
+
+For a composable string as above, define
+\begin{equation}
+ \begin{split}
+ U^a_m(sf_1,\ldots,sf_m)
+  =s\bigl(&\pi_{E_0}L_{E_0}(a)h_{E_0}\mathcal T(f_1)
+       h_{E_1}\mathcal T(f_2)\cdots\\
+       &{}\cdots h_{E_{m-1}}\mathcal T(f_m)i_{E_m}\bigr).
+ \end{split}
+ \label{eq:cc-components}
+\end{equation}
+There are \(m\) homotopies. For \(m=0\), the formula means
+\(U^a_0=s(\pi_EL_E(a)i_E)\).
+All domains in the formula match. Its parity as a suspended
+cochain is \(p+1\).
+
+\begin{theorem}[The coherent diagonal action]
+\label{thm:cc-coherent}
+For every homogeneous \(a\in B_W\),
+\begin{equation}
+ \mathfrak d U^a=U^{\delta a}.
+ \label{eq:cc-chain-map}
+\end{equation}
+Consequently a closed diagonal operator defines a central
+operation on the entire boundary category, with compatible
+homotopies at every arity. A diagonal boundary gives an exact
+Hochschild cochain.
+
+The construction is \(R\)-linear in \(a\) and \(k\)-multilinear
+in the boundary maps. Each positive-arity component vanishes
+if one boundary input is an identity. For a left scalar
+operator \(a=f(x)1\), its only component is
+\(U^a_0(E)=s(f(x)1_E)\).
+\end{theorem}
+\begin{proof}
+Write \(q_j=|f_j|\). First differentiate the unsuspended
+operator in \eqref{eq:cc-components}.
+Differentiating \(L(a)\) gives \(U^{\delta a}_m\).
+Differentiating \(\mathcal T(f_j)\) has sign
+\[
+ (-1)^{p+j+\sum_{i<j}q_i}.
+\]
+In \(-(-1)^{p+1}\widehat U^a b\), insertion of \(b_1\) at
+position \(j\) has sign
+\((-1)^{p+j-1+\sum_{i<j}q_i}\).
+These contributions cancel.
+
+Differentiating the homotopy immediately before \(f_j\)
+has sign
+\[
+ (-1)^{p+j-1+\sum_{i<j}q_i}
+\]
+and replaces that homotopy by \(1-i_{E_{j-1}}\pi_{E_{j-1}}\).
+For \(j\geq2\), the term \(1\) composes the two adjacent
+boundary maps. Its sign is opposite to the insertion of
+\(b_2\) in \(-(-1)^{p+1}\widehat U^a b\).
+For \(j=1\), move \(L(a)\) past \(\mathcal T(f_1)\) and use
+naturality of \(\pi\). The resulting left endpoint term has
+coefficient \((-1)^{p+pq_1}\).
+Insertion of \(U^a_{m-1}\) after \(sf_1\), followed by \(b_2\),
+has the opposite coefficient
+\((-1)^{(p+1)(q_1+1)+q_1}=(-1)^{pq_1+p+1}\).
+
+In a term containing \(-i\pi\), the factor
+\(\pi\mathcal T(f_j)h=f_j\pi h\) is zero unless \(j=m\).
+For \(j=m\), the surviving right endpoint has coefficient
+\[
+ -(-1)^{p+m-1+\sum_{i<m}q_i}.
+\]
+The composition
+\(b_2(U^a_{m-1}(sf_1,\ldots,sf_{m-1}),sf_m)\)
+has the opposite coefficient. This cancels the remaining term.
+The same calculation includes \(m=1\), where both endpoint
+terms occur at the only homotopy.
+For \(m=0\), \(\delta\pi=\delta i=0\) gives
+\(\delta(\pi L(a)i)=\pi L(\delta a)i\).
+Every component of \eqref{eq:cc-chain-map} is therefore proved.
+
+An identity at the last position gives \(hi=0\).
+An identity at an earlier position gives \(h^2=0\).
+For a scalar \(a=f(x)1\), each positive-arity formula starts
+with \(f(x)\pi h=0\). Its nullary value is \(s(f(x)\pi i)\).
+Every map outside the boundary inputs is \(R\)-linear, proving
+linearity in the left scalar coefficients of \(a\).
+\end{proof}
+
+The first compatibility equation recovers the homotopy of
+Section~\ref{sec:da-detection}. For closed \(a,f\), the unary
+equation is
+\[
+ \delta\bigl(\pi L(a)h\mathcal T(f)i\bigr)
+   =(-1)^{p+1}\bigl(F_1(a)f-(-1)^{p|f|}fF_1(a)\bigr).
+\]
+The higher components of \eqref{eq:cc-components} give its
+compatibility with arbitrary longer strings.
+
+\begin{example}[A nonzero binary homotopy]
+\label{ex:cc-binary}
+Take the two-variable boundary \(K(y,y^2+v^2)\) for
+\(W(x,u)=x^3+xu^2\), with the exterior order
+\(1,\theta_1,\theta_2,\theta_2\theta_1\) from
+Section~\ref{sec:da-mixed}. Let \(a\) be the even diagonal
+endomorphism sending \(\theta_2\theta_1\) to \(1\) and every
+other basis vector to zero. Then
+\[
+ U^a_2(s(u1_E),s(x1_E))=s1_E .
+\]
+This can be derived before any matrix multiplication.
+Write the contraction as \(h=h_2+i_2h_1\pi_2\), where the
+second coordinate is eliminated first.
+The first exterior block of \(i=i_2i_1\) is \(i_1\), independent
+of \(v\). Thus
+\[
+ h\,\mathcal T(x1_E)i=-i_2(\theta_1\,1_E).
+\]
+Its first block is \(-\theta_1\,1_E\). Multiplication by
+\(\mathcal T(u1_E)=v1\), followed by \(h_2\), gives
+\(\theta_2\theta_1\,1_E\), since
+\(({-v}-({-u}))/(u-v)=1\).
+The other term \(i_2h_1\pi_2\) vanishes: its input to \(h_1\)
+lies entirely in the \(\theta_1\)-summand.
+Applying \(a\) and then \(\pi\) gives the asserted identity.
+The operator \(a\) need not be closed. Its differential remains
+in \eqref{eq:cc-chain-map}, so this computation concerns the
+full cochain map, not only its values on cohomology.
+\end{example}
+
+\section{A scalar class recovered by compatibility}
+\label{sec:cc-cubic}
+
+Return to \(W=x^3\) and the boundary \(D\) in
+\eqref{eq:cc-cubic-opening}. For \(f\in k[x]\), let
+\(\rho(f)\) be the cochain whose nullary value at each boundary
+is \(s(f1)\), with all positive-arity components zero.
+Scalar multiplication is closed and commutes with every
+boundary map, so \(\mathfrak d\rho(f)=0\).
+
+\begin{theorem}[The cubic scalar classes]
+\label{thm:cc-cubic}
+For the category of all finite free matrix factorizations of
+\(x^3\), the scalar map
+\[
+ k[x]\longrightarrow HH_k^{\bar0}(\mathcal C),
+       \qquad f\longmapsto[\rho(f)]
+\]
+has kernel \((x^2)\). Thus its scalar classes retain both
+\([1]\) and \([x]\).
+The class \([x]\) acts by zero on the cohomology of every
+individual boundary endomorphism algebra.
+\end{theorem}
+\begin{proof}
+On the diagonal let
+\[
+ a=\frac13\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\2x+y&0\end{pmatrix}.
+\]
+Writing \(r=x-y\) and \(g=x^2+xy+y^2\), its differential is
+\[
+ \delta a=\frac13\bigl(g+r(2x+y)\bigr)1=x^2\,1.
+\]
+Theorem~\ref{thm:cc-coherent} gives
+\(\mathfrak dU^a=\rho(x^2)\).
+Multiplication of cochain outputs by any scalar polynomial
+commutes with \(\mathfrak d\). Every multiple of \(x^2\)
+is therefore exact.
+
+To prove the reverse inclusion, reduce a polynomial modulo
+\(x^2\), writing its remainder as \(\alpha+\beta x\).
+Suppose \(\rho(\alpha+\beta x)=\mathfrak dV\), where \(V\)
+has suspended parity zero. Restrict all components to the
+single boundary \(D\). Put \(V_0=sh\), so \(h\) is odd.
+The nullary equation gives
+\(\delta h=(\alpha+\beta x)1\).
+Write
+\[
+ h=\begin{pmatrix}0&b\\d&0\end{pmatrix}.
+\]
+Its differential is \(x(d+xb)1\). Evaluation at \(x=0\)
+forces \(\alpha=0\), and cancellation of \(x\) gives
+\(d+xb=\beta\).
+
+Evaluate the unary equation on the closed odd matrix
+\(e\) from \eqref{eq:cc-cubic-opening}.
+Write \(V_1(se)=sv\). Formula~\eqref{eq:cc-differential}
+gives
+\[
+ \delta v=he+eh=(d-xb)1=(\beta-2xb)1.
+\]
+Every entry of \(\delta v\) is divisible by \(x\).
+Evaluation at \(x=0\) now forces \(\beta=0\).
+This argument permits all higher components of \(V\):
+none enters its nullary or unary equations.
+It therefore excludes an arbitrary Hochschild primitive.
+
+Finally, Theorem~\ref{thm:pb-object-ideal} gives
+\(I_{\mathrm{obj}}(x^3)=(x)\).
+Equivalently, the polynomial block decomposition consists of
+contractible blocks and blocks \(K(x,x^2)\), \(K(x^2,x)\).
+On either noncontractible block, multiplication by \(x\) is
+the differential of an odd matrix. The homotopies combine
+over direct sums, proving the last assertion.
+\end{proof}
+
+The exactness of \(x^2\) also has a formula entirely on the
+boundary category. For a boundary matrix \(D_E(x)\), let
+\(D'_E\) denote entrywise differentiation in \(x\).
+The cochain \(U^a\) used in the proof has components
+\begin{equation}
+ U^a_0(E)=\tfrac13sD'_E,\qquad
+ U^a_1(sf)=-\tfrac13s f',\qquad U^a_m=0\quad(m\geq2).
+ \label{eq:cc-derivative-primitive}
+\end{equation}
+To obtain the first formula, the top-right entry of \(a\)
+selects the divided-difference matrix in \(i_E\), whose
+diagonal value is \(D'_E\).
+For the second, \(h\mathcal T(f)i\) has exterior component
+\((f(y)-f(x))/(x-y)\). Its diagonal value is \(-f'(x)\).
+For two or more inputs, \(h\mathcal T(f)h=0\) in one
+coordinate, because \(h\) takes values in the exterior summand
+and vanishes on that summand.
+
+Differentiating \(D_E^2=x^3\,1\) gives
+\(\delta(D'_E/3)=x^2\,1\).
+For any homogeneous boundary map \(f:E\to F\),
+\[
+ (\delta f)'=\delta(f')+D'_Ff-(-1)^{|f|}fD'_E.
+\]
+This is the unary equation for
+\eqref{eq:cc-derivative-primitive}. The binary equation is
+\((fg)'=f'g+fg'\), and every higher equation is zero.
+Thus the displayed primitive can be checked without the
+diagonal module.
+
+Its derivative component is \(k\)-linear but not \(R\)-linear:
+\((xf)'=f+xf'\). This is a mathematical reason to specify the
+base of the Hochschild cochains. The equations just proved
+concern \(HH_k\), rather than cochains required to be
+multilinear over \(R\).
+
+\section{Gradient relations and formal continuity}
+\label{sec:cc-gradient}
+
+For any polynomial potential in \(n\) coordinates, entrywise
+differentiation of \(D_\Delta^2=(W(x)-W(y))1\) gives
+\[
+ \delta(\partial_{x_j}D_\Delta)=(\partial_{x_j}W)1.
+\]
+Theorem~\ref{thm:cc-coherent} therefore supplies an explicit
+Hochschild primitive for every gradient scalar.
+The scalar map factors through
+\[
+ R/(\partial_{x_1}W,\ldots,\partial_{x_n}W)
+       \longrightarrow HH_k^{\bar0}(\mathcal C).
+\]
+This construction uses neither an isolated critical point nor
+an assumption about the dimension of the displayed quotient.
+The cubic theorem establishes injectivity for its scalar
+classes by a separate argument. A general isomorphism requires
+additional control of all Hochschild cochains.
+
+For formal series, use the complete boundary modules and
+total-degree filtrations of Section~\ref{sec:da-formal}.
+Restrict the cochain space to components continuous jointly
+in each fixed finite string of inputs.
+If \(a\) has coefficient order at least \(N_a\), and the
+entries of \(f_j\) have orders at least \(N_j\), then
+\eqref{eq:cc-components} has output order at least
+\[
+ \max(0,N_a+N_1+\cdots+N_m-m).
+\]
+Multiplications add orders, the \(m\) homotopies each lower
+order by at most one, and inclusions and projections preserve
+order. This estimate proves joint continuity.
+The differential of a cochain uses only finitely many
+components at each arity, so it preserves this space.
+All identities extend coefficient by coefficient.
+No sum of operations over infinitely many arities is needed.
+
+The diagonal now acts by coherent central operations on the
+boundary category. Identifying this cochain map with an
+equivalence of centres further requires control of its kernel
+and cokernel on all classes. Compatibility with products of
+bulk operators, cyclic pairings, and position-dependent chiral
+operations requires the corresponding comparison maps.
